(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-skin-hair-nail-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Skin, Hair & Nail Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"Before beginning the skin exam, the patient should be undressed and covered with a gown open in the back, and should also remove...","choices":["only their shoes","nothing; cosmetics do not need to be removed","makeup, nail polish, or other cosmetics","only jewelry, but makeup and nail polish may remain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Patient preparation includes removing makeup, nail polish, or other cosmetics that could obscure or mimic skin/nail findings, along with being gowned appropriately and considering a chaperone. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"The recommended patient positioning for the skin exam in this Physical Diagnosis I course is...","choices":["supine/prone exclusively, as taught in this course","lithotomy position","Trendelenburg position","seated/standing"],"answer":3,"exp":"While supine/prone positioning is preferred by some providers, the seated/standing position is specifically the method used and taught in this Physical Diagnosis I course. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"When examining the hair and scalp, the examiner begins by standing in front of the patient and...","choices":["only ever inspecting the scalp from directly behind the seated patient","separating the hair with fingers or a cotton-tipped applicator to visualize the scalp","immediately cutting a small hair sample for later microscopic laboratory analysis","skipping direct scalp visualization entirely and relying on palpation alone instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Scalp examination begins with the examiner standing in front of the patient and using fingers or a cotton-tipped applicator to part/separate the hair, allowing direct visualization of the scalp for lesions, dandruff, nits, and so on — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"During scalp examination, hair texture is best assessed by...","choices":["it cannot ever be assessed on a routine physical exam","palpating it as you part the hair during inspection","a distinct, separate percussion step performed afterward","brief auscultation performed with a stethoscope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hair texture is noted through palpation as the examiner parts the hair during the inspection process, integrating palpation into the same step as visual scalp inspection. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"Before uncovering any skin during the full-body exam, the examiner should...","choices":["skip informing the patient entirely, in order to save a bit of valuable clinical time","uncover the patient's entire body all at once, purely for the sake of overall efficiency","leave each area permanently uncovered once that particular portion of the exam is finished","ask permission/inform the patient what comes next, then only uncover the area being examined"],"answer":3,"exp":"The recommended approach is to inform the patient before uncovering any area, exposing only the skin currently being examined, and recovering that area with the gown once finished, before moving to the next area. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"When inspecting the chest and abdomen during the skin exam, a drape should be used to...","choices":["cover only the patient's face and upper neck area","gently clean the skin surface before inspection begins","replace the gown entirely for the rest of the exam","cover the patient's lower body while the gown is raised"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the gown is raised to inspect the chest/abdomen, a drape is used to keep the lower body appropriately covered, maintaining patient modesty throughout. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"During the full-body skin exam, inspection of all surfaces of the feet should specifically not forget to check...","choices":["nothing in particular; all foot surfaces are equally easy to inspect","the soles and interdigital spaces","the scalp again","only the top of the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Foot inspection specifically should not omit the soles and interdigital (between-toe) spaces, areas that are easy to overlook but where lesions (for example, fungal infections, melanoma) can occur. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"To examine the lower back and posterior legs as part of the full-body skin exam, the patient is typically asked to...","choices":["stand","remain supine throughout","sit with legs crossed","lie prone on the exam table"],"answer":0,"exp":"The patient is asked to stand for assessment of the lower back and posterior legs, allowing the examiner to inspect the lower back, posterior thighs, lower legs, and (with further exposure) the buttocks. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"When documenting a skin lesion, the examiner should describe its location, number, configuration, exudates, whether it is flat/raised/depressed, color, texture, shape, and...","choices":["the patient's blood type and Rh factor, noted separately","size, measured in millimeters (both long and short axis if not round)","the patient's insurance information and current billing account details","nothing beyond the lesion's overall color, per this documentation list"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lesion documentation includes size measured in millimeters — both the long and short axis if the lesion is not round — along with location, number, configuration, exudates, elevation (flat/raised/depressed), color, texture, and shape. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why is it important to measure both the long and short axis of a lesion that is not round, rather than a single diameter?","choices":["round lesions also technically require this exact same two-axis measurement approach","a single measurement would not accurately capture the true size/shape of an irregular or elongated lesion","it is not actually important, since a single measurement is essentially always sufficient","it only really matters clinically for lesions located specifically on the face"],"answer":1,"exp":"For a non-round lesion, a single diameter measurement could significantly under- or overstate its actual size or fail to capture its shape; measuring both the long and short axis gives a more complete, accurate description. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"Asking about sunscreen use and tolerance to sunlight ('do you burn easily, sometimes, or never?') as part of the history primarily helps assess...","choices":["the patient's cardiac risk factors exclusively","the patient's skin cancer risk and photoprotective behaviors","absolutely nothing that is clinically relevant","the patient's baseline auditory hearing function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sun tolerance and sunscreen use are directly relevant to assessing a patient's skin cancer risk (photosensitivity/burning tendency being a recognized risk factor) and their current photoprotective habits. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"Asking about changes in skin/hair/nail products or detergents/fabric softeners as part of the history is useful for identifying possible...","choices":["new peripheral visual field defects or subtle deficits noted","nothing of any real clinical relevance to this history at all","new-onset cardiac arrhythmias or irregular heart palpitations","contact dermatitis or an allergic/irritant reaction to a new product"],"answer":3,"exp":"A recent change in personal care products or laundry products is a common trigger for contact dermatitis (allergic or irritant), making this a useful history question when evaluating a new skin complaint. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"During the full-body skin exam, after inspecting the back, the examiner proceeds to inspect...","choices":["only the face again, needlessly repeating an already-completed inspection step","nothing further at all; the entire skin exam simply ends after the back","directly to the feet, entirely skipping the upper body, arms, and hands","all surfaces of the shoulders, axillae, arms, and hands, including the fingernails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Following inspection of the back, the systematic sequence moves to all surfaces of the shoulders, axillae, arms, and hands, including inspection and palpation of the fingernails. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"The full-body skin exam sequence, after inspecting the chest/abdomen, proceeds to inspect the...","choices":["anterior/medial/lateral thighs and lower legs","only the hands, skipping the legs entirely","scalp again, repeating the earlier step","nothing further; the exam is complete after the chest/abdomen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Following inspection of the chest and abdomen, the systematic sequence proceeds to the anterior, medial, and lateral surfaces of the thighs and lower legs, continuing the general head-to-toe organization. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"Toenail inspection and palpation, performed alongside inspection of all foot surfaces, notes color, shape, condition, and...","choices":["any lesions or other abnormalities","bowel sound character and pitch","lung sounds heard bilaterally","resting blood pressure reading"],"answer":0,"exp":"Toenail assessment, like fingernail assessment, notes color, shape, condition, and any lesions or other abnormalities present. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Considering having a chaperone present during the skin exam (a noted recommendation) is particularly relevant because the exam...","choices":["never requires any patient undressing at all, unlike most other physical exams","is always performed entirely with the patient remaining fully clothed throughout","has essentially no bearing whatsoever on the patient's overall comfort level","can require the patient to be significantly undressed/exposed across many body regions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because a thorough skin exam requires progressively exposing many body regions (often with the patient undressed in a gown), having a chaperone present is a reasonable consideration for both patient comfort and appropriate practice standards. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Washing hands before and after the skin exam (or using hand sanitizer) primarily serves to...","choices":["meaningfully improve the accuracy of visual inspection findings","entirely replace the separate need for gloves in every situation","have essentially no real purpose during this particular exam","reduce the risk of infection transmission between patient and examiner"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hand hygiene before and after the exam is a standard infection-control measure, reducing the risk of transmitting organisms between patient and examiner, especially relevant given the extensive skin contact involved in this exam. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Eccrine sweat glands, in contrast to apocrine glands, are characterized by...","choices":["near-total absence from nearly every area of the body's surface entirely","wide distribution across the body, opening directly onto the skin surface","opening exclusively into hair follicles, never directly onto the skin","limited distribution confined only to the axillae and groin regions specifically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Eccrine glands are widely distributed across the body and open directly onto the skin surface (important for thermoregulatory sweating), in contrast to the more anatomically limited apocrine glands (axillary/groin), which open into hair follicles. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Within the epidermis, the stratum corneum is specifically...","choices":["not a real anatomic structure","the deepest sublayer, adjacent to the dermis","located within the subcutaneous tissue","the most superficial sublayer"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stratum corneum is the most superficial sublayer of the epidermis, composed of dead, keratinized cells that form the skin's outermost protective barrier. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Terminal hair, in contrast to vellus hair, is characteristically...","choices":["fine and lightly pigmented, like 'peach fuzz'","found only in newborn infants and young toddlers","coarser and pigmented, as seen in scalp hair and eyebrows","identical in every respect to fine, unpigmented vellus hair"],"answer":2,"exp":"Terminal hair is coarser and pigmented, typical of scalp hair and eyebrows, in contrast to the fine, lightly pigmented vellus hair ('peach fuzz') found more widely across the body. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"The cuticle, a nail structure, functions to...","choices":["protect the space between the proximal nail fold and nail plate, and should be smooth and unbroken","protect only the lateral sides of the nail plate from everyday mechanical trauma","provide the nail plate's characteristic pink color via its underlying visible capillaries","form the pale white half-moon shape visible at the proximal edge of the nail bed"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cuticle protects the space between the proximal nail fold and the nail plate; normally it should appear smooth and unbroken, with disruption potentially indicating trauma or infection risk. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"A note in lecture reminds examiners that inspection of the breasts, as part of a complete dermatologic exam, may be performed...","choices":["at the end of the examination","only if the patient specifically requests it","never as part of a dermatologic exam","only at the very beginning, before anything else"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture specifically notes that breast inspection (as part of the overall dermatologic assessment of the chest) may be deferred to the end of the exam. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"Examination of the mucous membranes of the eyes, nose, and mouth, though part of a complete dermatologic exam, is specifically covered in...","choices":["no lecture anywhere in the entire course curriculum covers this specific content at all","this lecture exclusively, with absolutely no cross-reference found anywhere elsewhere","the separate Physical Examination of the Eye and HENT (Head, Ears, Nose, Throat) lectures","the separate abdominal and gastrointestinal exam lecture materials entirely instead"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rather than being covered in detail in this skin/hair/nail lecture, examination of the mucous membranes of the eyes, nose, and mouth is addressed in the separate Eye and HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lectures, even though it remains part of a complete dermatologic assessment overall. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Social history relevant to the skin/hair/nail exam includes occupation, hobbies/activities, alcohol/tobacco/drug use, diet, bathing/care routine, pets, living conditions, travel, and...","choices":["nothing beyond occupation","only insurance provider","cultural/religious practices","only the patient's blood type"],"answer":2,"exp":"The social history checklist extends to cultural or religious practices, which can influence skin/hair care routines and relevant exposures, alongside occupation, hobbies, substance use, diet, pets, living conditions, and travel. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Because sebaceous glands secrete their fatty product via hair follicles, and the palms/soles lack hair follicles, this anatomic relationship explains why...","choices":["eccrine glands are absent from the palms and soles","hair follicles are unrelated to sebaceous gland distribution","the palms and soles lack sebaceous glands","the palms and soles have more sebaceous glands than anywhere else"],"answer":2,"exp":"Since sebaceous glands are structurally associated with hair follicles (secreting through them) and the palms/soles are non-hair-bearing (glabrous) skin, the palms and soles correspondingly lack sebaceous glands. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"The skin's appendages — hair, nails, and sebaceous/sweat glands — are best understood as...","choices":["specialized structures derived from and associated with the skin, extending its function","completely unrelated organs with absolutely no embryologic connection to the skin at all","structures present only in certain rare individuals with specific inherited genetic conditions","structures found only deep within the subcutaneous tissue, never within the dermis itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hair, nails, and sebaceous/sweat glands are considered appendages of the skin — specialized structures that develop from and remain structurally/functionally associated with it, contributing to functions like protection, thermoregulation, and sensation. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Because skin color depends on multiple factors including melanin, hemoglobin (oxy- and deoxy-), carotene, and bilirubin, an examiner evaluating a color change should consider...","choices":["which of these several distinct pigment systems is most likely responsible, based on the color quality and distribution","only the underlying vasculature, deliberately ignoring melanin, carotene, and bilirubin altogether entirely","only melanin, since it is considered clinically to be the only pigment that genuinely ever matters","nothing in particular, since all skin color changes share one single, identical underlying cause always"],"answer":0,"exp":"Since several different pigments/mechanisms can each produce their own characteristic color change (for example, bluish from deoxyhemoglobin/cyanosis, yellow from carotene or bilirubin, brown from melanin), a thoughtful examiner considers the specific color quality and distribution to reason about which underlying mechanism is most likely. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Documenting whether a lesion is flat, raised, or depressed provides information about its...","choices":["the definitive underlying diagnosis of the lesion","elevation profile relative to the surrounding skin surface","the patient's current overall vital sign readings","nothing that is useful for clinical documentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"This descriptor specifically characterizes a lesion's elevation profile — whether it sits flush with, above, or below the surrounding skin surface — an important part of a complete lesion description alongside color, size, shape, and configuration. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"An abnormally increased nail base angle (approaching or exceeding 180 degrees, versus the normal ~160 degrees) is a described feature associated with...","choices":["improved nail health","digital clubbing","only cosmetic nail polish changes","a completely normal nail variant with no significance"],"answer":1,"exp":"An increased nail base (Lovibond) angle, approaching or exceeding 180 degrees rather than the normal ~160 degrees, is a recognized feature of digital clubbing, often associated with underlying cardiopulmonary conditions. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Adequate lighting is emphasized as very important for the skin exam primarily because it...","choices":["only matters specifically for the nail exam, not the general skin exam overall really","entirely replaces the separate need for careful, hands-on palpation of every lesion","allows accurate visualization of subtle color changes, lesion borders, and texture on inspection","is not actually all that important for this particular type of dermatologic exam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Since so much of the skin exam depends on visual inspection (color, lesion characteristics, texture, borders), adequate lighting is essential to avoid missing or misjudging subtle but clinically important findings. — Physical Examination of the Skin, Hair, and Nails lecture, Slide 24"}]};
